<Project>

  <!-- ============================================================
       Explains a known Visual Studio toolchain bug that otherwise
       shows up as an opaque NativeAOT publish failure.

       On a machine with the environment variable
       NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath set, `dotnet publish` for a
       PublishAot project can fail with MSB3073 and a corrupted
       CppLinker value containing text like:

         'vswhere.exe' is not recognized as an internal or external
         command, operable program or batch file.

       Chain of causes (verified 2026-08-25, machine has the variable
       set): the ILCompiler targets locate the native C++ toolchain by
       running vcvarsall.bat, which calls VsDevCmd.bat. VsDevCmd.bat
       pushd's into the Visual Studio Installer folder and invokes
       vswhere.exe by its bare name, relying on cmd.exe resolving it
       from the current directory. NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath
       disables exactly that resolution, so the lookup fails and
       writes an error to stderr. findvcvarsall.bat redirects only
       stdout, and the ILCompiler targets' ConsoleToMSBuild capture
       folds that stderr text into the CppLinker property. The
       resulting error blames a missing platform linker on a machine
       where the linker is installed and correct.

       This is a Visual Studio bug, not a FalkForge bug. This target
       only detects and explains the condition; it never clears or
       overrides the variable itself. It is a deliberate
       machine-hardening setting, and silently working around a
       security control to make a build convenient is the wrong move
       here.

       Scoped narrowly so it never fires on an ordinary solution
       build or on any of the other 60+ projects:
         - only for a project that sets PublishAot=true (only
           FalkForge.Engine and FalkForge.Engine.Elevation import
           this file)
         - only on Windows (the bug is in cmd.exe's search behavior)
         - only when this is an actual publish, gated on
           $(_IsPublishing), the SDK's own marker for a real publish
           operation (set true by `dotnet publish` or `dotnet pack`
           on a PackAsTool project, empty on a plain `dotnet build`).
           BeforeTargets is
           set to both "Build" and "Publish": measured empirically
           (2026-08-25) that hooking "Publish" alone never ran here,
           most likely because `dotnet publish -r <RID>` on this SDK
           re-evaluates the project through an inner MSBuild
           invocation for RID resolution, and only the "Build" hook
           reliably lands inside that inner evaluation, right after
           the inner Build completes and before the native link step
           that actually fails. The $(_IsPublishing) condition is
           what keeps this off a plain `dotnet build`, confirmed by
           the same measurement: $(_IsPublishing) is empty during
           `dotnet build` and "true" during `dotnet publish`.
         - only when the environment variable is actually set on this
           machine
       ============================================================ -->

  <Target Name="_FalkDiagnoseAotLinkerEnv" BeforeTargets="Build;Publish"
          Condition="'$(PublishAot)' == 'true' AND '$(_IsPublishing)' == 'true' AND '$(OS)' == 'Windows_NT' AND '$(NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath)' != ''">
    <Warning Text="FALKAOT001: NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath is set on this machine, which triggers a Visual Studio toolchain bug during NativeAOT publish (vcvarsall.bat -&gt; VsDevCmd.bat calls vswhere.exe by bare name, relying on current-directory search that this variable disables). vswhere.exe then fails to resolve, and the resulting stderr text gets folded into the linker path, so the publish fails with MSB3073 blaming a &quot;missing&quot; platform C++ linker that is actually installed and correct. This is a Visual Studio bug, not a FalkForge one. Workaround: clear the variable for this build session only, then publish, e.g. in PowerShell: $env:NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath = $null ; dotnet publish. Do not disable it machine-wide; it is a deliberate hardening setting." />
  </Target>

</Project>
